# The paddle. One frame, and deliberately not a nine-slice.
#
# **The paddle cannot change width.** `game::PADDLE_HALF_WIDTH` is a `const`
# and nothing in `game.rs` shadows it — no power-up, no difficulty curve, no
# per-life shrink — so the paddle is 10 world units across on every tick of
# every run. A nine-slice would buy a stretch nothing ever asks for, at the
# price of caps whose size has to be defended against `expand`'s minimum. When
# a widening power-up arrives, this becomes `nine: 12 12 0 0` and the body
# stretches between two fixed ends; until then the honest art is one picture at
# the size it is drawn.
#
# 100 x 6 texels at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 10 is
# `2 * PADDLE_HALF_WIDTH` x `2 * PADDLE_HALF_HEIGHT` = 10 x 0.6 world units:
# the collider, exactly.
#
# Six texels of height is not much to draw in, so it is spent on the three
# things that read at this size: a dark rim, one row of highlight under the top
# edge, and a shaded row above the bottom. The four white texels in the middle
# mark the centre, which is information the player uses — `game::bounce` turns
# the ball by where along the face it lands.
crpix: 100 6 1 5 1

palette:
  . c None
  k c #12222b
  w c #eaf6ff
  c c #4fb0e6
  d c #2b6f99

frame paddle:
  ..kkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkk..
  .kwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwk.
  kcccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccwwwwccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccck
  kcccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccwwwwccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccccck
  .kddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddddk.
  ..kkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkkk..

sample: pixel
